      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to determine if there are changes in laboratory investigations which allow to recognize coagulation disturbances associated with inhaled glucocorticoid therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "The study group consist of 26 patients with stable asthma (5 male, 21 female) without risk factors of venous thrombosis, mean age 45 +/- 14 years. 15 patients were treated with 500-1000 mg beclomethasone daily, 11 patients received a daily dose of 1000-2000 microg of beclomethasone. The laboratory investigations included: hemoglobin concentration (Hb), hematocrit (Ht), platelet count (PLT), red blood cell count (E), white blood cell count (L), percentage of eosinophils (Eos%), fibrinogen (Fib), D-dimer concentration, activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT), prothrombin time (PT), prothrombin index (Quick), INR and von Willebrand factor (vWF)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "An elevated concentration of D-dimers was detected in 4 patients (15%) the others investigated coagulation parameters were within normal range. We found a significant correlation between the dose of inhaled glucocorticosteroid and the PT, prothrombin index and INR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The results revealed no evidence of increased fibrinolytic activity in subjects with stable bronchial asthma treated with inhaled glucocorticosteroids."
        }
      ]},
